[Music]  this morning we are celebrating a  cultural phenomenon unlike any other it  has been 50 years since soul train first  crossed the tracks of our minds the show  premiered back in 1971 as a showcase for  black musical talent and influenced  everything from dance to dress and even  ad spending the broadcast which ran for  35 seasons remains one of the longest  running syndicated shows in american  history and continues to inspire  generations of artists across genres  i joined music and culture journalist  and author nelson george at rough trade  records in new york city to discuss how  soul train came to be and its enduring  legacy  [Music]  it's been half a century since soul  train rolled onto the scene and into the  history books here's your host don  cornelius chicago dj don cornelius  created the show to highlight black  artists largely ignored by the  mainstream charts gotta remember there  is no national black shows showcasing  black music you could have you know the  supremes on that sullivan but that's a  one shot journalist and filmmaker nelson  george is author of soul train the  hippest trip in america don's i did was  kids and big name acts just black music  the show was an instant hit in chicago  and relocated to los angeles to be  broadcast nationally soul train show  number 154 but cornelius was an unknown  in hollywood the biggest challenge he  had without the booking talent who is  this guy he's another black dj from the  midwest unable to land big name acts on  the west coast soul train turned to  local tower a move that would give the  show its signature when you think of  soul train we know it's black music  but the stars are the show of the  dancers originally known as the soul  train gang the dancers changed the way  america learned to move before then  dance traveled almost word of mouth what  soul train did was take the kind of an  anecdotal way that black dance traveled  and put it on a national thing so things  like pop popping locking all these  different dance styles they go from  soul train into every school yard in  america in one day when the jackson five  appeared and did dancing machine  and michael got up there  and did the robot  the next day  every kid is out there like oh yeah yeah  do the robot but when did  the musicians all of a sudden realize  you got to be on soul train well i think  one of the big gets was james brown  [Music]  because james brown was the guy and once  brown said it was okay it helped open up  the floodgates how about a gang from  miss aretha franklin stevie wonder the  moments fully guaranteed  [Music]  [Applause]  hey soul train was the national  vehicle for black music from 1971  untouched into the 90s  but more than just a music dance variety  show soul train depicted black life like  never before i always say that black joy  was the weapon the civil rights movement  didn't know it had you're not seeing  them marching and protesting you're  seeing the fun part of black culture so  this was a window in the black culture  that had never been ever on american tv  [Music]  and decades later soul train is still  celebrating the best in seoul and r b  with the b-e-t soul train awards  [Music]  we caught up with grammy-nominated  singer-songwriter leon bridges during  rehearsals for the award show at the  world famous apollo theater in harlem  growing up my family was super religious  but they made an exception  for  soul music my father you know he he put  me on to the og soul train you know and  so curtis mayfield  [Music]  he created the blueprint of what r b  guitar is you know fast forward to when  you know i picked up a guitar from the  first time i tried to  just kind of channel that energy this  year's awards are celebrating soul  train's 50th anniversary and the apollo  is the perfect backdrop says award show  executive producer connie orlando all  artists were like we got to do that  stage we have to do the apollo stage we  have to do the soul train stage because  when you went there it was home some of  the greatest  soul artists and r b artists have graced  that stage and so i feel like there's a  little bit of you know lingering magic  everybody  and soul trains magic born out of an  idea that music and moves of black  culture should be celebrated and chanted  everyone into understanding that this is  america i would say that don built a  platform people say solo train line  everyone gets happy it's just america  it's just americana  it's part of the culture  i'm don cornelius and as always in  parting we wish you love  peace  and soul  and like don used to say if you didn't  get it it can't be got  that is soul training i tell you my  girlfriends being in la  my friends in high school would go down  stand in line to try and get  into the soul train taping and there was  only one who did angelique [ __ ] into the  division she made it in  legend dance like no other it wasn't  just black artists who performed  david bowie played on soul train that  was huge i mean elton john was one of  the biggest stars in the world still is  and for him to play soul train it was  huge it was a huge moment and we were  all talking about it all all morning as  we've been saying i remember it coming  on tv and you remember that moment and  seeing the train 11 a.m right 11 87 am  that's what i remember would come on and  i that was me my uncle and my aunt they  were like teenagers and we all went out  the next day everybody was like  your robot was good what wedding doesn't  have a soul train line right everywhere  every wedding everybody gets happy all  right so the 2021 so  training a little higher pitch  dana bringing it way better than not  good that's at 8 pm eastern and pacific  on b-e-t of course the division of icon  cbs and head to cbsmornings.com and our  social media feed for an extended  behind-the-scenes look i joined the  apollo's executive producer camila  forbes to discuss the theater's history  and continuing cultural impact yeah  she's a good one you know her

